it is\0A// still writing to. A child meant to outlive its scope needs a design\0A// decision this module has not made yet; see DESIGN.md.\0A//\0A// Output is readable ONLY once the child has exited. Reading it early\0A// would mean defining what a partial read of a file somebody is still\0A// writing means, and nothing needs that yet.\0Aextern \22C\22 {\0A    fn process_spawn(program: CStr, args: CStr, argc: Int, env: CStr, envc: Int, input: CStr, input_len: Int, has_input: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_capture_status(h: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_poll(h: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_child_wait(h: Int, timeout_nanos: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_terminate(h: Int, hard: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_child_pid(h: Int) -> Int;\0A    fn process_child_free(h: Int);\0A}\0A\0A/// A running child process.\0A///\0A/// **Killed and reaped when this value dies** — on a normal return, at\0A/// the end of a block, on an `Err`, and on a panic. Once the handle is\0A/// gone nothing can poll the process, wait for it, signal it or read\0A/// its output, so leaving it running would leak something the program\0A/// can no longer name.\0A///\0A/// Its output is readable only after it has exited, and then any number\0A/// of times.\0Apub handle Child { on_drop: process_child_free }\0A\0A/// Every length is bound BEFORE any `as_cstr`, which is the ordering\0A/// `Net.write` and `File.write` both spell out: the pointer is an\0A/// interior one into the string's cell, and reading a length afterwards\0A/// in the same argument list is how that bug was found on the wire.\0A/// Named `start`, not `spawn`: `spawn` is a KEYWORD, the\0A/// concurrency construct, so `Process.spawn(..)` does not parse at\0A/// all. Making it parse would mean teaching the parser that a\0A/// keyword after `.` is a member name, which is real grammar work\0A/// for a naming preference. `start` also pairs with `wait`.\0Apub let start (opts: Options): Result[Child, String] = unsafe {\0A    let joined = join_args_a